{
  "name": "How to Get Seamless Kitchen Trim Installation",
  "step": [
    {
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "Getting that seamless look for your kitchen trim isn't as hard as it seems, but it definitely takes a methodical approach. First, always dry-fit your trim pieces before you secure them. This lets you spot any fit issues with uneven walls or cabinets and make adjustments proactively. Next, ensure you're using proper fastening methods, like brad nails or screws, to securely attach filler strips, end panels, and crown molding to a nailing cleat if you have frameless cabinets. After everything is firmly in place, you'll need to meticulously fill all nail holes and caulk any seams. This step is crucial for achieving that smooth, finished appearance. Remember, trim work usually happens after your cabinets are installed but before final painting, as the finishing touches need to be done first. If you're dealing with complex areas like uneven walls or intricate crown molding, that's when a professional really shines, ensuring precise scribing and mitering for a truly flawless result. Taking these steps seriously will ensure your kitchen trim looks like it was always meant to be there.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1
    }
  ],
